Ethereum, EVM, solidity, smart contract explanation:
====================================================

Refined explanation:
--------------------

👉 A blockchain = a network of computers + shared program they all run together.

A blockchain is a system/network made up of:

- Many computers (called nodes), and

- A shared program (i.e, blockchain software) that all these computers agree to run together.


* For ethereum, there is a shared program (software) — e.g., Geth or Nethermind — that defines Ethereum’s rules.
And it is installed and run by all the computers in the network.

Also, each computer has a copy of the same data and runs the same programs and thus follows the same rules.
Any instruction sent by a single computer in a network is checked and verified by all to make sure that it is fair and correct.

No single computer is the boss — they agree collectively on what’s true and this establishes the trust.


Next, inside ethereum software, we have a subsystem called EVM (i.e engine + rulebook).
it’s the engine that actually runs smart contracts and ensures every computer reaches the same results.
It mostly defines rules for how the smart contract code runs inside that etherum system — execution, gas, and storage behavior.

It defines:

- How to read and execute Solidity code (the programming language),
- How to calculate transaction fees (gas),
- How to store and change data safely,
- How to produce identical results on every node, no matter where in the world it runs.


Solidity:
---------
It is the language developers use to write instructions for the EVM to tell ethereum what to do.
These instructions describe how things should work — for example:
"If someone sends 1 Ether, give them a digital ticket."

The EVM then reads those instructions and executes them exactly.

********
Ethereum is a system whose brain is EVM. 
we tell the EVM what specific tasks to perform inside the system — i.e., we write smart contracts that run within Ethereum’s existing system.
And solidity is the language in which we write instrutions but instructions itself is called smart contract.
******

🧠 In One Line

Ethereum is the body,
the EVM is the brain,
Solidity is the language the brain understands,
and smart contracts are the actual thoughts (instructions) that the brain executes.


Smart contract:
--------------
These are the instructions we write to perform specific tasks in ethereum blockchain.
Like, If someone sends 1 Ether, give them a digital ticket.

So it is like an agreement  meaning it defines what should happens when certain conditions are met.

When those conditions become true, the code automatically executes the result —
no one can stop it, delay it, or change it.

💡 That’s what makes it “smart.”

It can enforce itself without human help.

They are called Smart Contracts because they act like contracts (define rules and agreements) but are smart enough to execute themselves automatically using code.

| Concept            | Meaning                                                                    |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Smart Contract** | A program on the blockchain that executes rules automatically when called  |
| **Function Call**  | The trigger that starts execution (done manually or by another contract)   |
| **Smartness**      | The code enforces rules *without human intervention* after being triggered |
| **Manual Trigger** | Just the starting signal — not manual control over execution               |

A smart contract:

Automatically executes instructions once "triggered" manually by frontend/other smart contract.

But does not spontaneously start itself (except if another contract or automated bot calls it).


So, “smart” means:
Once triggered, the contract acts on its own, following rules you wrote — no middlemen, no manual approvals, no bias.
Everything happens based on code + math, not trust + people.

It cannot be changed or stopped once deployed — it always does what it was programmed to do.

We don’t tell the EVM how the Ethereum system works;
we tell the EVM what specific tasks to perform inside the system — i.e., we write smart contracts that run within Ethereum’s existing system.


🧩 Why There Are Two Sets of Rules:
------------------------------------
Ethereum is made up of layers, and each layer has its own job.
Each layer needs its own rules so that everything works together in an organized way.

It is like an analogy of a "city and the engine".
Imagine Ethereum as a "city" and the EVM as the engine inside every "car" in that city.

🔹 City Rules = Ethereum Rules (High level rules of how the system should work)

These are the rules that govern how the whole city runs:

- How roads are built (blocks)
- How traffic moves (transactions)
- How traffic lights work (consensus)
- How citizens communicate (peer-to-peer network)
- How people get rewards for maintaining the city (validators, staking)

These are the system-level rules — the full structure that makes the city function safely and fairly.



🔹 Engine Rules = EVM Rules (Smart contract level rules)

Now, inside the city, all cars (smart contracts) run on the same type of engine (the EVM).
The EVM has its own mechanical rules, such as:

- How to start the engine (run a contract)
- How much fuel it needs (gas fees)
- How the engine turns inputs into movement (how code executes)
- How every car behaves identically when given the same command (deterministic execution)

These are code-execution-level rules, not city-wide rules.

💡 The EVM is like a subsystem inside Ethereum, responsible specifically for running code.
Both are rules of Ethereum, but they belong to different layers of the system.

🧠 Simple Summary

- Ethereum rules define how the blockchain as a whole works — blocks, transactions, consensus, communication, and rewards.

- EVM rules define how the smart contract code runs inside that system — execution, gas, and storage behavior.

- They are different because they serve different purposes:
	- Ethereum rules keep the "network running" smoothly,
	- EVM rules keep the "code running" correctly.



All computers in ethereum has same data
----------------------------------------
* Ethereum is a big ledger or record book.

It keeps track of things like:

- Who owns how much Ether,
- What smart contracts exist,
- What transactions have happened.

Every computer in the Ethereum network has a copy of this record book — from the very first page to the latest one.

So if one computer’s record book gets damaged or someone tries to cheat,
the others can say:

“Wait, our copies don’t match yours — you’re wrong.”


All computers in ethereum has same program
------------------------------------------

Ethereum isn’t just storing data — it also runs programs, which are the smart contracts.
Every computer in Ethereum has the EVM (Ethereum Virtual Machine) inside it.
When someone sends a transaction (for example, using a smart contract),
all computers run the same program to check the result.

Example:

- A smart contract says: “If someone sends 1 Ether, give them 1 digital ticket.”
- You send 1 Ether.
- Every computer runs the same code (via the EVM).
- They all get the same answer: “Yes, give Arjun 1 ticket.”
- Because all computers agree, the action becomes official.

All computers run the same code so they get the same results and agree on the truth.


EVM supported chains
---------------------
- They all understand the same language(solidity) and programs as Ethereum.

That means:

- If you write a smart contract in Solidity (for Ethereum),
- You can copy-paste it onto Polygon or BSC,
- And it will work the same way, because they also use the EVM (Ethereum Virtual Machine).

💡 Analogy:
Think of Ethereum, Polygon, and BSC like different computers running the same operating system (like Windows).
The programs (smart contracts) work the same on each because they understand the same “language”.

💡 So, if you can build on Ethereum, you can easily build on Polygon or BSC — that’s why they’re called EVM-compatible chains.
So frontend libraries(web3.js, ether.js, wagmi, viem etc ) and ethereum blockchain languages or libraries can work the same way in EVM supported chains also.



🔸 Polygon and BSC — “Modified versions” of Ethereum
=====================================================
Original code:
-------------
Originally blockchain was written for ethereum. 

Since it was open-source, so it was "Copied and modified" from Ethereum.

Creators of polygon & BSC(binance smart chains) changed a few parts of that code to make it work differently. And thus we have different blockchains but they work the same way and are EVM supported.

* Ethereum = thousands of computers running the “Ethereum rules.”

* Polygon = hundreds of computers running a modified version of Ethereum’s rules (faster, cheaper).

* BSC = computers (mainly run by Binance) running another modified version of Ethereum’s rules.

💡 They’re different networks, but their “engines” (EVM) and “language” (Solidity) are the same — that’s why they’re compatible.

Ethereum is the main network — the original, most trusted one.
Polygon and BSC are side networks or alternative networks — they’re like helpers that make things cheaper and faster.
You can move your money or tokens between them using a bridge.


💡 Example:
If a smart contract costs 5€ to run on Ethereum,
it might only cost 5 cents on Polygon — but it’s slightly less secure.

- Different speeds, prices, and levels of security for evm supported chains.

