- It calculates how much you’ll get, charges a small fee, and executes the trade.

🧮 Example:

You swap 1 ETH for 2000 USDC (minus 0.3% fee).
The smart contract updates the pool balances automatically.



7️⃣ Liquidity Mining / Yield Farming

Once you provide liquidity, some protocols also reward you with extra tokens (like UNI or ALGB) for participating.

It’s a way to earn passive income on your crypto.

🧠 Think: “Deposit tokens → Earn trading fees + reward tokens.”


8️⃣ Staking

- Locking your tokens into a smart contract to support the network or earn rewards.
- Different from liquidity providing (no swapping involved).
- You can stake governance tokens (like UNI) to earn yield or vote on proposals.





USDT Token presence on a specific network:
============================================
•	A token like USDT exists on a network only if the corresponding smart contract (the ERC-20 contract that defines USDT’s logic) is deployed on that specific network.
•	Each blockchain (Ethereum, Polygon, your custom EVM, etc.) needs its own deployment of the USDT contract.
So:
•	If the USDT contract is deployed on your devnet, then you can use USDT there.
•	If it’s not deployed on devnet, then USDT “doesn’t exist” there — you’d either deploy a mock version or test on mainnet, where the contract is deployed.

In short:

Tokens are not “global”; they are bound to the smart contract that defines them on each specific network.

