Explanation of depositMoney() function in above contract:
--------------------------------------------------------

Initial function code:

function depositMoney() public payable {
    balances[msg.sender].totalBalance += msg.value;

    Transaction memory deposit = Transaction(msg.value, block.timestamp);

    balances[msg.sender].deposits[balances[msg.sender].numDeposits] = deposit;

    balances[msg.sender].numDeposits++;
}

Now the above deposit function :
- Needs to have value for totalBalance (coming from "msg.value").
- Needs to have "numDeposits" value (populated by taking its already stored value, initially is 0) as:
	balances[msg.sender].numDeposits // gives its already stored value.

- Need to store "Transaction" value corresponding to "numDeposits" key in "deposits" mapping like:
	"deposits": { "numDeposits" :  "Transaction"}

- Then need to increment this value by 1 so that next deposit can happen.


Also, in above we see that "balances[msg.sender]" is repeated 4-5 times in above code 
so we can optimise this :

- by a "storage" keyword

	Balance storage userBalance =  balances[msg.sender];

	- "storage" keyword will be a "reference" or "another name" for the same blockchain location 
		"balances[msg.sender]" and thus can easily change anything inside that location with new variable name. 
		It is a good approach to "extract reusable memory locations" inside a variable name this way.

- Can not use a "memory" keyword;

	Balance memory userBalance =  balances[msg.sender];

	- "memory" is only temporary and last till the execution of function. Later it will not exists.

Final optimisd code:
--------------------
function depositMoney() public payable {
    Balance storage userBalance =  balances[msg.sender]; // another name for same memory location

    userBalance.totalBalance += msg.value;

    Transaction memory deposit = Transaction(msg.value, block.timestamp);
    userBalance.deposits[userBalance.numDeposits] = deposit;
    userBalance.numDeposits++;
}


