🧠 Goal:
“The wallet should be able to receive funds, no matter what.”
--------------------------------------------------------------
This sounds simple, but in Solidity, receiving ETH can happen in different ways depending on:

* Whether the sender included data
* Whether the contract has a matching function
* Whether the contract spends more than 2300 gas
* Whether the sender uses .call(), .transfer(), .send(), or a contract fallback
* Whether someone uses selfdestruct(address)

To guarantee "all these ways work", you need to use the correct special functions.

✅ 1. The two functions used


receive() external payable {
    emit Deposit(msg.sender, msg.value);
}

fallback() external payable {
    if (msg.value > 0) {
        emit Deposit(msg.sender, msg.value);
    }
}

----------------------------------------------------
These are Solidity’s built-in “catch-all” functions.
----------------------------------------------------


📌 2. What is receive()?

receive() is a special function that is triggered:

👉 When ETH is sent to the contract "without any data"

Examples:

✔ Sending ETH from MetaMask

* You open MetaMask
* Enter contract address
* Press “Send”
* No data attached → triggers receive()


✔ Using low-level calls

(bool success,) = wallet.call{value: 1 ether}("");

- Also has empty data, so it triggers receive().


✔ Using .transfer() or .send()

wallet.transfer(1 ether);
wallet.send(1 ether);

Both send a plain ETH transfer → triggers receive().


🔍 Why do we need receive()?

Because without it:

* ETH sent without data would revert
* Most normal users sending funds from a wallet app would fail
* MetaMask → Send ETH → Would fail
* .transfer() / .send() from a contract → fails
* Low-level .call{value: …}("") would call fallback(), but fallback must be payable

* If fallback isn't payable → FAIL

So adding a dedicated receive() is the most correct and safe way to ensure simple ETH transfers work.


📌 3. What is fallback()?

fallback() is triggered when:
👉 A function is called that doesn’t exist

wallet.notAFunction(123);

👉 ETH is sent with data and no matching function exists


🔍 Why do we need fallback()?

So a payable fallback ensures:

* ETH + data? → accepted
* Unknown function call? → no revert
* Interaction with smart contracts → safe


=======================
.transfer(): Forwards only 2300 gas.

It means:
when you use the built-in Solidity function .transfer() to send Ether (ETH) from one contract or address to another, the EVM strictly limits the amount of gas accompanying that transaction to exactly 2,300 gas units.
This is a hardcoded limit within the Solidity compiler for the .transfer() and .send() functions.
=======================


🧠 Goal:
“It is possible for the owner to spend funds on any kind of address, no matter if its a so-called Externally Owned Account (EOA - with a private key), or a Contract Address.”
--------------------------------------------------------------

The normal withdraw or transfer function in smart contract that we write is this:

function transfer(address payable _to, uint _amount) public {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "You are not the owner. Aborting.");
    _to.transfer(_amount);
}

The code specially " _to.transfer(_amount)" or this function ".transfer()" CAN ONLY work with EOAs wallets
but not with contract accounts.

Meaning it can only transfer funds to EOAs(externally owned accounts) but will fail to transfer funds to 
contract accounts/addresses.

It is because ".transfer()" or ".send()" function which is used to send from our current contract to another contract. 
If the other Smart contract that receives Ether via .transfer() attempts to do anything more than simply logging the transaction will likely run out of gas and fail.
When we transfer from our smart contract to another EVM limits the gas to 2300 only and most of the operation except logging needs more than 2300 gas and will likely REVERT and that is why it will fail and those methods are not a good or ideal or recommended to transfer to another contract.


Why .transfer() and .send() are not recommended
================================================
* They work reliably only with normal wallets (EOAs).
* They often fail when sending ETH to smart contracts.

Reason:
-------
.transfer() and .send() both "limit the gas for the receiving contract to 2300 gas".
That’s a very small amount of gas, only enough for very simple actions.

Most smart contracts need "more than 2300 gas" to run their receive() or fallback() logic 
(e.g. writing to storage, emitting events, complex checks).

So when they receive ETH via .transfer()/.send(), they often:

* run out of gas
* revert
* and the ETH transfer fails

Because of this, .transfer() and .send() are not reliable for sending ETH to contracts.

The modern recommended way is:

==============================================
(bool success, ) = _to.call{value: amount}("");

require(success, "Transfer failed");
==============================================

- call{}() does not enforce the 2300 gas limit, so it works with both EOAs and smart contracts.



⛽️ Why the 2,300 Gas Limit?
-----------------------------
- The 2,300 gas limit was deliberately introduced as a security measure to protect contracts from a specific type of attack called a reentrancy attack .
- More on this later


The contract function looks like this:

function transfer(address payable _to, uint _amount, bytes memory _payload) public returns(bytes memory) {
    
    require(msg.sender == owner, "You are not the owner. Aborting.");

    (bool success, bytes memory data) = _to.call{value: _amount}(_payload);

    require(success, "Aborting! Call was not sucessfull!.");

    return data;
}

Why do we pass a _payload in the transfer function?
----------------------------------------------------
Because .call() can do more than just send ETH — it can also trigger a function on the target contract.

* If _to is a normal wallet (EOA) → _payload is ignored, and ETH is simply sent.

* If _to is a smart contract → _payload tells the contract which function to run and what arguments to pass.

This makes your wallet more powerful: it can pay ETH and interact with contracts in the same transaction, such as:

- calling a function on a DeFi protocol
- buying an NFT
- interacting with a DEX
- triggering a fallback function with custom data


Why is _payload typed as bytes memory?

Because:

1. Function calls are encoded in bytes

Smart contract function signatures and arguments must be ABI-encoded.

Example:
myFunction(uint256 x, address y) becomes a sequence of bytes.

.call() accepts its data only as raw bytes <===========
.call{value: _amount}(_payload) requires the input to be bytes.<======

bytes memory is the most flexible type

It allows:

- empty payload ("")
- encoded function calls
- arbitrary data for fallback functions


🧠 Goal:
"It should be possible to allow certain people to spend up to a certain amount of funds." or
"Owner can give spending allowances to other addresses (like “mini-limits”)"
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- The concept is called "allowance".

- Allowance can be set for any address which is not "owner".

✅ What the allowance system does:
- It allows certain people/addresses to "spend money" upto a "certain limit" set by the owner of the wallet.

Example:
“John can spend 1 ETH from my wallet.”
“Maria can spend 0.2 ETH only.”

This requires tracking:

1. How much each person is allowed to spend :
	
	mapping(address => uint) public allowance;

2. Whether they are allowed at all :

	mapping(address => bool) public isAllowedTosend;


That’s why we have two mappings.

mapping(address => uint) public allowance;
mapping(address => bool) public isAllowedTosend;

✔ Together, they form:

- permission + limit
Both are needed for safe behavior.

Example:
allowance[John] = 1 ether;

This means John can spend maximum 1 ETH through the wallet.

Example:
isAllowedTosend[John] = true;

Means John is allowed to spend (but the amount is in the allowance mapping).


Why do we need this boolean?
----------------------------
Because a non-owner should not enter the spending branch unless:

- they are approved (boolean = true)
- AND they have allowance (amount > 0)

Otherwise someone with 0 allowance could still enter but fail deeper in the function.

So the boolean acts as a quick permission check.

Flow of allowance system:
-------------------------
1. First setAllowance() is called to give someone "permission" 
	and "set the limit" of funds allowed to spend.

	- Only the owner can call this function:
	require(msg.sender == owner);

Then the function:

- stores the spending limit in:
	 allowance[_for]

- updates allowed/not allowed in 
	isAllowedTosend[_for]

If the owner sets the amount to 0:

* allowance[John] = 0

* isAllowedTosend[John] = false

→ John is no longer allowed at all.

2. Next, .transfer() function can be used by other contract to spend its allowance if previously set.
  .transfer() function in the current code is interesting as it allows non-owner to call it and 
  "reduce its allowances" from blockchain mapping whatever they are transferring out to them.

  Also it checks if they have permission or are trying to transfer more than they are allowed by owner.

Step-by-step:

1. If caller is not owner → we go into the allowance logic.

2. First check:
isAllowedTosend[msg.sender]
→ ensures they have permission at all.

3. Second check:
allowance[msg.sender] >= _amount
→ ensures they don’t exceed their spending limit.

4. After successful sending:
allowance[msg.sender] -= _amount;
→ reduces the remaining allowance.

So spending looks like:

* BEFORE: John has 5 ETH allowance
* John calls transfer(..., 2 ETH)
* AFTER: John has 3 ETH allowance left

✅ Summary

* allowance[address] = how much ETH the address can spend
* isAllowedTosend[address] = whether the address is allowed at all
* setAllowance() sets both the permission and the maximum limit
* transfer() checks both before sending ETH
* Non-owners must respect allowance; owner bypasses it & can transfer any amount.
* .call{value: _amount}() performs the actual transfer


