How to call smart contract from web3 library on frontend
==========================================================

- Any frontend library like :-
	* web3.js
	* viem
	* ether.js

can be used to interact with smart contracts from frontend.

All of these libraries need 2 important things:
	- ABI (Application binary interface) array
	- contract address

Note:
-----
- Ask "ABI array" fronm smart contract developer as it is generated while deploying so only a smart contract developer can provide you this.
Without it you can not interact with contract.


ABI array contains the name of the functions, its arguments type, output type.
The ABI Array contains all functions, inputs and outputs, as well as all variables and their types from a smart contract.


Compilation of smart contract:
------------------------------

When you compile a smart contract then you get "bytecode" which gets deployed. 

Inside there are assembly opcodes telling the EVM during execution of a smart contract where to jump to.

Those jump destinations are the first 4 bytes of the keccak hash of the function signature.

Best is, we’re looking through the lens of the debugger into the execution of a smart contract and run through that.



🌟 Why do frontend libraries need the ABI even if you specify the exact function name?
=======================================================================================

Because the blockchain doesn’t understand human-readable function names.
It only understands encoded byte data (called calldata).
The ABI is required to translate your human-friendly function call into that byte data.

frontend libraries require the ABI because the blockchain only accepts encoded byte data—ABI tells the library how to encode your function call and decode its response; your JS function call alone does not provide that information.



Events in solidity
